Here's how everything fits together in Compass:
Two request sources: The Compass UI and external apps are the two entry points. The UI path is richer — it runs through a RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) pipeline that pulls in metric definitions, news articles, and writing guidelines to enrich the prompt before sending it to the LLM. External apps, by contrast, pass a prompt_id to identify which prompt template to use, and Compass resolves the template and calls the LLM directly — no RAG, just clean API-style execution.
Prompt management lives as a dedicated layer with its own CRUD endpoints (POST /prompts, GET /prompts/{id}, PUT /prompts/{id}, DELETE /prompts/{id}). Every prompt has a prompt_id, a template body (with variable slots like {{metric_value}}, {{risk_type}}), version history, and metadata. When an external app calls Compass, it sends its prompt_id + runtime variables; Compass fetches the template, injects the variables, and fires the LLM call. When the UI calls Compass, the resolved prompt also gets enriched by RAG context before the LLM sees it.
RAG pipeline (UI-only): the user's metric context triggers three retrieval paths — a vector DB lookup for metric definitions, a news/context search, and a guidelines store — all three chunks get merged and appended to the system prompt as context before generation.
Multi-app, multi-prompt support: Each external app (App A, App B, App C...) can maintain multiple prompt_ids for different commentary styles (brief summary, detailed narrative, risk-adjusted tone, etc.). They just pass the right prompt_id at call time.
